speechtext:
When this Bill was up before, I drew the attention of the minister to the great destruction of bar fish in the St. Lawrence river. I do not see anything in this Bill relating to this particular matter, but I would like to know whether the department has taken any steps to prevent the destruction of the bar fish. It seems to me that some very simple mode could be adopted. I have drawn the attention of Prof. Prince and other officials of the department to the great destruction of that fish. Thousands are taken every year of a. very small size that is almost not fit for fcod, and I think something should be done to prevent this scandalous destruction of one of our finest fishes. I think the department should take action. There are two or three ways in which it could be done. The size of the fish to be caught could be limited or the weight. I know that in the province of Quebec there is a conflict of authority between the provincial fishery authorities and the federal authorities, but it seems to me that something ought to be done. It is really a very strong grievance;
I would almost say it was a national disaster to have that fish destroyed by the thousands every year. It is not utilized

for any purpose. The peddlers simply go from fishery to fishery, and are even ashamed to go to the market, but the fish is distributed on the highway and in the back parishes. I wish the minister would see whether he could do something to prevent the destruction of that very valuable fish.
